I can't do a formal proof, but it's a fairly easy thought exercise. 
This will prove that the set of infinatly long numbers between 0 and 1 
containing only the digits 3 and 7 is a larger set then the set of all 
integers greater then 0.
Pick two random digits (I'll use 3 and 7).
Now make a list that assigns each integer to a unique number that is 
between 0 and 1, composed only of the digits 3 and 7, and contains an 
infinate number of digits.
So you'll get a list that looks something like this:
1 - 0.373777373...
2 - 0.773337373...
3 - 0.333377737...
4 - 0.773377373...
5 - 0.377333773...
6 - 0.373733777...
7 - 0.333333333...
8 - 0.777777777...
9 - 0.373737373...
. - ..............

So you've basically generated an infinatly long list that shows that 
each integer can be matched to a unique number between 0 and 1 composed 
only of the digits 3 and 7. You can now make a number that won't appear 
on the list. Do do this, all you have to do is have the nth digit of 
that number be the opposite of the nth digit of the nth number of the 
list (Sorry if this is unclear it's easier to explain this in person 
where you can just draw circles. "Opposite" means that 3 will become 7, 
and 7 will become 3).

So with the list I just had, you would take the number I bracked:
1 - 0.[3]73777373...
2 - 0.7[7]3337373...
3 - 0.33[3]377737...
4 - 0.773[3]77373...
5 - 0.3773[3]3773...
6 - 0.37373[3]777...
7 - 0.333333[3]33...
8 - 0.7777777[7]7...
9 - 0.37373737[3]...
. - ................
and get "0.373333373...", you would then reverse the digits to 
"0.737777737..." which will not appear anywhere in the list. This shows 
that the set of infinatly long numbers between 0 and 1 containing only 
the digits 3 and 7 (which I'm sure you'll admit is a pretty small 
subset of all the numbers between 0 and 1) is a larger set then the set 
of all integers greater then 0, even if they both do happen to contain 
an infinate number of elements.

